  [38;5;153mindex.html[39m changes one line: the script tag gets [38;5;153mtype="module"[39m.

  [1mOne decision inside this section: the file extension.[0m The repo has two module systems already — [38;5;153mapp.js[39m is browser
  globals, [38;5;153msrc/[39m is CommonJS. New code has to be ESM (the browser needs it, and injected deps want real imports). Two
  ways to get there:

  - [1m[38;5;153m.mjs[39m extensions[0m (what I've written above) — Node and the browser both read these as ESM with no [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m change,
    and [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m / [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m are never touched. The cost is mixed extensions in one directory.
  - [1m[38;5;153m"type": "module"[39m in [38;5;153mpackage.json[0m — uniform [38;5;153m.js[39m everywhere, but it reclassifies the whole package, so the existing
    [38;5;153mgreet[39m demo's [38;5;153mrequire[39m calls break and I'd have to convert those two files too.

  I recommend [38;5;153m.mjs[39m. The [38;5;153mgreet[39m demo is unrelated to this feature, and converting it is exactly the kind of collateral
  change that makes a diff hard to review. Mixed extensions are ugly but honest about which files are new.

  Also worth flagging: [38;5;153mapp.js[39m currently registers its submit handler at load time with no DOM-ready guard. [38;5;153mtype="module"
[39m  defers execution until after parsing, so this actually gets [3msafer[0m — but it's a behavior change, and I'd rather name
  it than have it look accidental.

  Does this layout look right so far?

